1) Lock the research design

Outcomes: pick 3–5 primary (e.g., violent, property, burglary, robbery).

Treatment: an indicator for Republican governor (or Democrat, pick one as baseline). Add event-time dummies for an event-study.

Units & time: city-year as the main panel; optionally state-year for a cleaner aggregate.

Sample rule: keep only cities ≥85k (your README says you already restricted to 28 cities). 
GitHub

2) Data audit & build

Start from city_year_Iowa_Illinois_Michigan_1973_1991.csv as the main table; keep monthly_raw_... only if you later want seasonality/AR terms; keep state_group_year_... for state-year robustness. 
GitHub

Create a tidy panel: city_id, state, year, outcome(s), population, socio-econ controls (poverty, income, racial comp., density) where available.

Merge governor party by state-year and exact turnover years for IL/MI and the “no change” path for IA (as per README). 
GitHub

3) Identification & specs (baseline → event-study)

TWFE (city, year FE):
y_{cst} = β * RepGov_{st} + γ_c + δ_t + X'_{cst}θ + ε_{cst}
Cluster SEs at state (conservative) or use two-way (city & year) if you prefer.

Event-study (pre-trends):
y_{cst} = Σ_{k≠−1} β_k 1[years since switch = k] + γ_c + δ_t + X'_{cst}θ + ε_{cst}
Plot β_k with CIs; check pre-period coefficients ~ 0.

Staggered DiD correction (optional but good): apply Sun & Abraham / Callaway & Sant’Anna estimators since IL/MI switch at different times. (In R: fixest::sunab() or did package.)

4) Robustness & falsification

Alt outcomes: offense-specific categories.

Alt samples: drop one state at a time; raise threshold (≥100k) to see if big-city subsample drives results.

Alt trends: state-specific linear trends; or region-year FE if you expand later.

Placebos: pretend the switch happened 3 years earlier; expect null.

Weights: pop-weighted vs unweighted.

Inference: wild bootstrap clustered by state (given only 3 states, report caution).

5) Interpretation & write-up

Summarize sign/magnitude in % terms relative to the sample mean.

Discuss mechanisms (budgeting, policing directives) as hypotheses, not proof.

Be explicit about limits: only 3 states; governance is not randomly assigned.

